1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,877
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet is the first end-to-end pipeline that
reads onboard camera and LiDAR and</font>

2
00:00:05,957 --> 00:00:08,060
<font color="#000000">predicts a sparse set of polylines</font>

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:10,371
<font color="#000000">the primitive planners actually use.</font>

4
00:00:10,451 --> 00:00:14,480
<font color="#000000">It skips rasterization entirely and sets a new
state of the art.</font>

5
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:19,663
<font color="#000000">Self-driving cars need HD maps marking lanes,
boundaries, and crosswalks.</font>

6
00:00:19,743 --> 00:00:24,345
<font color="#000000">Today these are annotated by hand, which is costly
and does not scale.</font>

7
00:00:24,425 --> 00:00:27,824
<font color="#000000">Learning methods instead predict a dense pixel grid,</font>

8
00:00:27,904 --> 00:00:32,640
<font color="#000000">but it carries no individual elements and needs
brittle post-processing.</font>

9
00:00:33,020 --> 00:00:37,580
<font color="#000000">The authors argue mapping should happen online,
from the car's own sensors,</font>

10
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:41,416
<font color="#000000">and the model should output the final
representation directly.</font>

11
00:00:41,496 --> 00:00:45,437
<font color="#000000">Prior methods rasterize then vectorize with a
hand-designed step;</font>

12
00:00:45,517 --> 00:00:49,520
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet removes that detour and predicts
geometry end to end.</font>

13
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:52,490
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet makes three contributions.</font>

14
00:00:52,570 --> 00:00:56,118
<font color="#000000">It predicts vectorized outputs directly from sensors,</font>

15
00:00:56,198 --> 00:00:58,925
<font color="#000000">with no rasterization or post-processing.</font>

16
00:00:59,005 --> 00:01:04,676
<font color="#000000">It uses the polyline as one flexible primitive
whose vertex order encodes direction.</font>

17
00:01:04,756 --> 00:01:09,810
<font color="#000000">And it adapts detection transformers to locate
elements in bird's-eye view.</font>

18
00:01:10,190 --> 00:01:12,111
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet has three stages.</font>

19
00:01:12,191 --> 00:01:18,846
<font color="#000000">A bird's-eye-view extractor maps each modality into
a shared space: cameras through a ResNet and IPM,</font>

20
00:01:18,926 --> 00:01:22,247
<font color="#000000">LiDAR through PointPillars, fused by concatenation.</font>

21
00:01:22,327 --> 00:01:27,515
<font color="#000000">A detection transformer with element queries
locates each element as keypoints.</font>

22
00:01:27,595 --> 00:01:31,849
<font color="#000000">An autoregressive polyline generator then emits
ordered vertices,</font>

23
00:01:31,929 --> 00:01:34,850
<font color="#000000">trained with detection and generation losses.</font>

24
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:38,930
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet is evaluated on nuScenes and Argoverse2.</font>

25
00:01:39,010 --> 00:01:44,922
<font color="#000000">Following HDMapNet, predicted polylines are
compared to ground truth for crosswalks,</font>

26
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:47,418
<font color="#000000">lane dividers, and road boundaries.</font>

27
00:01:47,498 --> 00:01:53,695
<font color="#000000">It reports Chamfer-distance AP and a new
Fréchet-distance AP that respects vertex order,</font>

28
00:01:53,775 --> 00:01:55,050
<font color="#000000">plus 3D evaluation.</font>

29
00:01:55,430 --> 00:01:58,923
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet sets a new state of the art.</font>

30
00:01:59,003 --> 00:02:04,587
<font color="#000000">It beats HDMapNet by 14.2 mAP on nuScenes and 14.6
on Argoverse2.</font>

31
00:02:04,667 --> 00:02:10,512
<font color="#000000">Gains hold across sensors: nearly eighteen points
camera, ten LiDAR,</font>

32
00:02:10,592 --> 00:02:12,342
<font color="#000000">over fourteen fusion.</font>

33
00:02:12,422 --> 00:02:17,570
<font color="#000000">Two-stage fine-tuning pushes fusion to 53.7 mAP
versus 31.0.</font>

34
00:02:17,950 --> 00:02:19,402
<font color="#000000">Two ablations stand out.</font>

35
00:02:19,482 --> 00:02:24,830
<font color="#000000">Representing each element by a bounding box with
two keypoints beats the alternatives</font>

36
00:02:24,910 --> 00:02:27,703
<font color="#000000">by two Fréchet and over seven Chamfer points.</font>

37
00:02:27,783 --> 00:02:32,556
<font color="#000000">Two-stage training, teacher forcing then
fine-tuning on predicted keypoints,</font>

38
00:02:32,636 --> 00:02:35,940
<font color="#000000">adds about seven mAP for camera and eight for fusion.</font>

39
00:02:36,320 --> 00:02:41,922
<font color="#000000">To summarize: VectorMapNet exceeds the previous
state of the art by 14.2 mAP on nuScenes and 14.6 on Argoverse2.</font>

40
00:02:42,002 --> 00:02:47,605
<font color="#000000">Its best configuration, fusion with fine-tuning,</font>

41
00:02:47,685 --> 00:02:53,287
<font color="#000000">reaches 53.7 mAP where HDMapNet reaches 31.</font>

42
00:02:53,368 --> 00:02:58,970
<font color="#000000">Gains span sensors: about eighteen for camera, ten
for LiDAR, fourteen for fusion.</font>

43
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:03,618
<font color="#000000">The takeaway: HD mapping needs no
rasterize-then-vectorize detour.</font>

44
00:03:03,698 --> 00:03:08,230
<font color="#000000">Treating mapping as detection plus autoregressive
polyline generation,</font>

45
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:12,117
<font color="#000000">VectorMapNet produces directional vector maps from
sensors,</font>

46
00:03:12,197 --> 00:03:16,860
<font color="#000000">beats rasterized pipelines by double digits, and
extends to centerlines.</font>
